<template>
  <v-main>
    <NavBar></NavBar>
    <v-container>
      <div class="m-auto m-3 col-md-8">
        <h2>
          Revisiting Instrumental Interaction Theory: Embodied Instruments with
          Generative Content
        </h2>

        <p class="serif-para py-3">
          Designers' reactions to the "smart" pen in FusAIn suggest that,
          despite its AI capabilities, they understood it primarily through the
          familiar metaphor of a pen. This observation resonates with a long
          tradition of pen-based interaction research, where new computational
          capabilities are layered onto the pen while preserving its role as a
          direct instrument for action. Across decades of pen-based interaction
          research, augmented pens have repeatedly expanded the range of
          qualities that can be manipulated through drawing- and sketching-like
          interaction. Systems such as Sketchpad, Conté, Vignette, filtered.ink,
          and I/O Brush each attached new computational capabilities to the pen
          while preserving its role as a direct and gesture-based instrument.
          FusAIn's "smart" pens belong to this lineage. However, unlike prior
          augmented pens that drove deterministic operations on stable digital
          materials, "smart" pens guide the synthesis of new visual content.
        </p>

        <h2>What should be reified with AI?</h2>
        <p class="serif-para py-3">
          Since the design of the "smart" pens directly draws upon
          Beaudouin-Lafon's theory of instrumental interaction, FusAIn can be
          analyzed through this lens. In FusAIn, each pen carries a set of
          visual properties (its logical component), while the physical pen and
          tablet provide the input device (its physical component). Drawing with
          the pen transforms a designer's gesture into a contextual generative
          command. Each smart pen reifies the command of "apply this visual
          property/material to this region of the canvas," and the designer can
          pick up and compose with it. The whole canvas, or the region being
          brushed over, serves as the domain object and responds visibly to
          these actions.
        </p>

        <p class="serif-para py-3">
          From an instrumental interaction perspective, a smart pen is not
          fundamentally different from traditional digital drawing tools: in
          both cases, the instrument mediates between the user and the domain
          object — the user acts on the instrument, the instrument transforms
          that action into operations on the canvas, and the resulting visual
          changes provide feedback. The key difference lies in the nature of the
          feedback: whereas conventional instruments invoke deterministic
          transformations on stable digital representations, smart pens invoke
          generative processes whose outcomes are synthesized rather than
          directly computed from a predefined function. This shift raises a
          central design question: what forms of reification best support
          interaction with generative content? More specifically, if generative
          feedback cannot be specified and predicted with the same precision as
          deterministic operations, what should be reified as an instrument to
          help users achieve desired outcomes?
        </p>

        <p class="serif-para py-3">
          Riche et al. approached this question through the concept of
          AI-Instruments, proposing the reification of "expressing any intent in
          natural language." In this formulation, the instrument engages with
          situated intention, capturing what a user wishes to achieve in a
          particular context at a particular moment. FusAIn adopts a different
          approach: while the underlying technical execution remains generative,
          its instruments engage with materials and visual qualities that
          professionals already understand and work with in their existing
          workflows without AI. Thus AI-Instruments and FusAIn both commit to
          reification in an AI context, but with different entities. The
          tradeoffs between the two approaches arise not from differences in
          technical execution, but from the representational choices embedded in
          what is made into an instrument.
        </p>

        <p class="serif-para py-3">
          I thus define Material-Instruments (with GenAI), in contrast to
          AI-Instruments, as follows:
        </p>

        <p class="serif-para py-3">
          <strong>Material-Instruments.</strong><br />
          Material-Instruments encode transferable knowledge. They capture
          material qualities, techniques, aesthetic concepts, and design
          vocabularies that remain meaningful across users, projects, and
          contexts.
        </p>

        <p class="serif-para py-3">
          <strong>AI-Instruments.</strong><br />
          AI-Instruments encode situated intentions. They capture what a
          particular user wants in a particular context at a particular moment,
          making their meaning dependent on the evolving circumstances of use.
        </p>

        <p class="serif-para py-3">And they differ in:</p>

        <p class="serif-para py-3">
          <strong>Transferability of Knowledge.</strong><br />
          AI-Instruments primarily externalize situated intentions. Their value
          comes from capturing what a user wants in a particular context, making
          them highly expressive for individual exploration but less naturally
          transferable across users. Material-Instruments instead externalize
          design knowledge in terms of material qualities and aesthetic concepts
          that are already shared within design practice. A "soft velvet" pen
          can be understood, reused, and adapted by different designers without
          requiring access to the original design rationale. The distinction
          therefore concerns not simply reusability, but whether an instrument
          encodes situated intent or transferable knowledge.
        </p>

        <p class="serif-para py-3">
          <strong>Persistence Across Time.</strong><br />
          Intentions evolve as projects evolve. Consequently, AI-Instruments are
          inherently provisional representations that may need to be
          continuously reconstructed as a designer's goals change.
          Material-Instruments encode qualities that persist across multiple
          projects and design situations, which can accumulate into longer-lived
          repertoires of reusable tools. This distinction reflects two
          complementary forms of support: one emphasizes responsiveness to
          emerging intentions, while the other emphasizes the accumulation of
          durable knowledge.
        </p>

        <p class="serif-para py-3">
          <strong>A new representation layer or an existing one?</strong><br />
          Material-Instruments build upon representational categories already
          present in design practice. Designers routinely reason about texture,
          color, materiality, and form, making these qualities natural
          candidates for reification as interactive tools. AI-Instruments
          instead reify intentions themselves, introducing a new
          representational layer that designers do not ordinarily manipulate
          directly. While this abstraction may enable new forms of control and
          reflection, it also requires designers to adopt new ways of
          conceptualizing and interacting with their work. The distinction is
          therefore not only technical but epistemic: whether a tool amplifies
          existing design vocabularies or introduces new ones.
        </p>
      </div>
    </v-container>
  </v-main>
</template>

<script>
import NavBar from "@/components/ui_components/NavBar.vue";
import { nextTick } from "vue";
export default {
  name: "revisit-instrument",
  components: { NavBar },
  props: {
    src: String,
  },
  data() {
    return {
      lazySrc: null,
      observer: null,
    };
  },
  mounted() {
    nextTick(() => {
      const el = this.$refs.videoRef;
      if (!el) return; // Safety check

      this.observer = new IntersectionObserver(
        (entries) => {
          entries.forEach((entry) => {
            if (entry.isIntersecting) {
              this.lazySrc = this.src;
              el.play().catch(() => {});
              this.observer.disconnect();
            }
          });
        },
        { threshold: 0.5 }
      );

      this.observer.observe(el);
    });
  },
  beforeUnmount() {
    this.observer?.disconnect();
  },
};
</script>

